/* Mo's primitives: the library functions written in C (docs/design/bytecode.md, section 6).
 *
 * The checker, the code generator, the verifier and the VM all read this table, and none of
 * them keeps its own copy. docs/design/library.md says where every function and operator of
 * the library is implemented, and make library-map checks that it has a row for every line
 * here; tests/test_tables.c checks the lines themselves. Define PRIM before including the
 * file, which undefines it at its end:
 *
 *     #define PRIM(id, module, name, signature, params, bugs) ...
 *     #include "base/prims.def"
 *
 * id: the primitive's name in C: its module and its name in capitals, without the name's % or
 * ?, as LIST_APPEND for List.append. A name ending in ? may add IS_, as LIST_IS_EMPTY does for
 * empty?. No two lines share an id.
 *
 * module and name: the checker adds the primitive to the module, as if it were declared there
 * under the name (bytecode.md 6). The module is that of the primitive's type, through which a
 * call such as Time.parse(text) names it (reference 10.5). Reference 5.1 would let two lines
 * of a module share a name if their first parameters were different types declared in that
 * module, but each module of the library declares only its own type. So no two lines share a
 * module and a name, and the bytecode's text form can write a primitive as Module.name, as in
 * List.append. A name that starts with %, which no Mo name can (reference 2.3), marks a
 * primitive that lowering emits and no Mo program can call: for literals, interpolation, text
 * forms, order, BigInt's operators, and the details of a failed assertion. Core's names made by
 * lowering start with % too (formal/TRANSLATION.md, section 0).
 *
 * signature: the primitive's type in Mo's syntax, with the type parameters T, U, K and V as the
 * reference writes them, and then, after where, the abilities it needs of them, as a
 * declaration names them (reference 5.5). A Map(K, V) or a Set(T) already needs K or T to have
 * Hash (reference 3.8), so map and set primitives don't repeat it. A constant, such as
 * Set.empty, has no parameters, and its signature is just its type.
 *
 * params: one letter for each parameter: o (owned) when the primitive consumes the argument,
 * b (borrowed) when it only reads it (bytecode.md 6). A parameter is owned when the primitive
 * returns that collection or text changed, built in its memory when it has one holder
 * (bytecode.md 4.6), as append, set and trim do; or when the primitive stores the argument in
 * its result, as append stores its element. Every other parameter is borrowed, and so is every
 * parameter of an immediate type, for which ownership means nothing (bytecode.md 4.2).
 *
 * bugs: the bugs the primitive can raise (reference 13.2), in the reference's words, separated
 * by "; ", or "" for none. Any primitive that allocates can also run out of memory, which isn't
 * listed.
 *
 * The lines follow docs/design/library.md, type by type. */

#ifndef PRIM
#error "Define PRIM(id, module, name, signature, params, bugs) before including base/prims.def."
#endif

/*   id                         module       name                  Mo signature                                  params bugs */
PRIM(INT_ABS,                   "Int",       "abs",                "fn(Int) : Int",                              "b",   "overflow")
